
Step 1: Check System Requirements

Make sure your computer meets the minimum hardware requirements before installing:
• Operating System: Windows (64-bit 10 or 11), macOS, Linux, or ChromeOS.
• Memory (RAM): At least 8 GB of RAM (16 GB is recommended for a smooth experience).
• Storage: At least 8 GB of free disk space (more is needed if you install multiple emulators and SDK components).

Step 2: Download the Installer

• Go to the official Android Studio Install Page.
• Read and accept the Android Software License Agreement.
• Click the download button to save the installer file (roughly 1 GB to 1.3 GB) to your computer.

Step 3: Run the Setup Wizard

• Launch the installer: Double-click the downloaded file (.exe on Windows, .dmg on Mac) and grant administrator permissions if prompted.
• Follow the wizard: Click Next through the setup steps.
• Select components: Ensure both Android Studio and Android Virtual Device (AVD) are checked if you want to use the built-in phone emulator.
• Choose install type: When the initial launch wizard starts up, select the Standard setup type.
• Download SDK components: Allow the wizard to download the necessary Android SDK, emulator tools, and APIs (this requires an active internet connection and can take a few minutes depending on your speed).
• Finish: Click Finish once all components are unpacked and installed.

Step 4: Create Your First Project

• Open Android Studio and select New Project.
• Choose a template (such as Empty Activity or Navigation UI Activity).
• Select your programming language (Kotlin or Java) and minimum SDK version, then click Finish to build your first project workspace.
